        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Nok culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, in present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaduna-state">Kaduna State</a> and the neighbouring areas around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jos-plateau">Jos Plateau</a>; the village of Nok, which gave the culture its name, lies in southern Kaduna State</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1500 BCE to around the turn of the Common Era: Early Nok c. 1500–900 BCE, Middle Nok c. 900–300 BCE (the height of the terracottas and the first iron), Late Nok c. 300 BCE onwards</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Hollow terracotta heads and figures with pierced eyes, elaborate hairstyles and jewellery; early iron smelting; pearl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> farming; small hamlets without towns</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taruga">Taruga</a>, Samun Dukiya and Pangwari, above all the work of Goethe University <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> since 2005; radiocarbon and thermoluminescence dates; plant remains and food residues in pots; no writing and no ancient accounts</span></div>
        </div>
