    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Nok culture, named after the village in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> where its first sculpture was found in 1928, lasted from about 1500 BCE to around the turn of the Common Era.</li><li>From about 900 BCE its people made hollow clay figures of men and women with pierced eyes, elaborate hairstyles and jewellery, among the earliest sculpture known south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</li><li>Nok farmers grew pearl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and smelted iron by the mid-first millennium BCE but lived in hamlets, not towns; looting for the art market has since ravaged most known sites.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first Nok sculpture was found in 1928 about 7 metres down in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mine, and a head found in 1943 served for a year as a scarecrow in a yam field before Bernard Fagg recognised it.</p></aside>
    </section>

