    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Farming communities settle in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>: the Early Nok phase begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 900 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Middle Nok phase opens; the first terracotta sculptures are made</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 8th–6th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">Iron smelting</a> begins in the Nok region, by current estimates (the dates are debated)</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 300 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Late Nok phase begins, known from only a few sites</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1 CE</span><span class="rf-text">Around the turn of the era the terracottas and the Nok settlements vanish from the record</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1928</span><span class="rf-text">Colonel Dent Young finds the first Nok terracotta in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mine near the village of Nok</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1943</span><span class="rf-text">A head used as a scarecrow leads Bernard Fagg to study the culture</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2005</span><span class="rf-text">Goethe University <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> begins long-term excavations of Nok sites</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Nok culture is named after Nok, a village in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaduna-state">Kaduna State</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, near which the first of its sculptures came to light. In 1928 Colonel Dent Young, a partner in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>-mining firm, found a terracotta sculpture about 7 metres down in alluvial tin workings there and gave it to the museum of the Mines Department in Jos. In 1943 a terracotta head, which had stood for a year as a scarecrow in a yam field, came to the attention of Bernard Fagg, a colonial administrative officer who had studied archaeology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. Fagg recognised its likeness to the find from Nok, gathered further pieces from the mines and devoted much of his career to the culture.</p><p>Because most early finds came from tin workings, where erosion and running water had moved them from where they were first laid down, the culture was hard to date. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taruga">Taruga</a> and Samun Dukiya, two sites where sculptures lay undisturbed, gave early radiocarbon and thermoluminescence dates, and older accounts usually placed the culture between about 500 BCE and 200 CE. Since 2005 a long-term project of Goethe University <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, led by Peter Breunig and Nicole Rupp, has surveyed and excavated many sites and built a new chronology from radiocarbon dates and the study of tens of thousands of potsherds. It divides the culture into Early Nok, c. 1500–900 BCE, Middle Nok, c. 900–300 BCE, and Late Nok, from c. 300 BCE to around the turn of the Common Era. On this view the sculptures first appear about 900 BCE, and those dated so far were made between roughly 2,900 and 2,000 years ago. The details are still debated, not least because looters have taken so many sculptures from the ground without record.</p><p>Where the Nok people came from is unknown, and nothing of their language survives. Breunig and Rupp have argued that, since the crops they grew had been domesticated elsewhere, the first Nok farmers must have moved into central Nigeria from outside; others have suggested that they came, like the farmers of the Gajiganna culture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> basin, from the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. The Early Nok settlers are known mainly from their pottery; the sculptures belong to the Middle and Late phases.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/millet"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millet.jpg" alt="Millet" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/millet">Millet</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panicum_miliaceum0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kurt Stüber [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Nok people lived in small rural settlements. Excavations have found hamlets and single compounds about the size of a farmstead today, on open plains, on hilltops and on the slopes of hills, but no towns or larger centres. Their houses were evidently built of wood, grass and other perishable materials and have left almost nothing behind; what survives is mostly pits filled with pottery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>, grinding stones and fragments of sculpture. Most of the sites dated so far belong to the Middle Nok phase, when the culture seems to have been at its most widespread.</p><p>Plant remains recovered from these pits show how the Nok people lived. Charred grains show that they grew pearl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, a cereal first domesticated in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, together with cowpea, and they gathered wild plants, among them the fruits of trees, which they may have tended around their settlements. Grinding stones show that grain was ground into flour, probably to be cooked as porridge. A chemical study of residues in Nok pots, published in 2021, found beeswax as well as animal fats: the Nok people were gathering honey about 3,500 years ago and used their pots for meat as well. Because the acid soils of the region have destroyed almost all bone, little is known of the animals they kept or hunted, and almost nothing of their bodies, their health or their numbers.</p><p>Of rulers, laws or social ranks nothing is known directly. The sculptures show men and women with elaborate hairstyles and abundant jewellery, which may mark people of high standing, and making them required skill, quantities of clay and fuel and, probably, specialists. Yet the settlements have yielded no palaces or monumental buildings, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> excavators have suggested that Nok society grew complex in its rituals rather than in the size of its settlements or the reach of its rulers. If so, Nok shows that an ambitious art could flourish among farming hamlets without cities or a state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No texts and no living tradition record what the Nok people believed, so everything said about their religion is inferred from the sculptures and from the places where they were found. In excavations the terracottas almost never stand where they were first set up. Most survive as broken fragments, scattered by erosion or gathered in pits and deposits, sometimes layered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>, and a large figure is only rarely found whole. Looting has made the problem worse: most sculptures now in collections were dug up without any record of their context, which is the evidence most needed to understand them.</p><p>The excavations have also found pits containing carefully made pottery and necklaces of stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> but no surviving bones, which have been interpreted as graves whose bodies have decayed in the acid soil. With the charcoal and the deposits of sculpture, these finds have been read as signs of an elaborate treatment of the dead. Interpretation remains cautious: stone settings that were first described as a megalithic enclosure were later recognised as natural rock formations.</p><p>The figures themselves have been explained in several ways: as images of ancestors, as markers of graves, as charms to protect crops and to ward off infertility and illness, and, since some have dome-shaped bases, as finials on roofs. None of these explanations has been proved, and the figures may have served different purposes in different places and centuries. Their care for hairstyle and adornment, their solemn faces and the large size of their heads relative to their bodies suggest images of respected persons rather than scenes of everyday life, but whom they represent is unknown. Animals appear among the sculptures too, far less often than people, and their meaning is equally uncertain. Comparisons with the ancestor cults and clay shrine figures of later West African peoples are often made, but across two thousand years they can suggest possibilities, not prove them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nok-terracottas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nok-terracottas.jpg" alt="Nok terracottas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nok-terracottas">Nok terracottas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Africa_Nok_Head_Kimbell.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FA2010, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The terracottas are the culture’s great achievement. They range from small figures to heads and bodies close to life size, and they show men and women standing or seated and, much more rarely, animals. They were made of coarse-grained clay, built up by hand in coils around a hollow interior, coated with a slip and burnished, and fired at low temperatures in open fires fed with grass, twigs and leaves. Their style is distinctive and remarkably consistent. The heads are large in proportion to the bodies; the eyes, usually triangular or semicircular beneath arching brows, have pierced pupils, and nostrils, ears and mouths are often pierced as well. Hairstyles are modelled in great detail, with buns, crests and plaits, and many figures wear necklaces, bracelets and anklets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>. Their combination of strict stylisation with lifelike detail has made them widely admired, and they are counted among the masterpieces of early African art.</p><p>Making such large hollow figures without cracking them in the firing demanded great skill, and the number of fragments points to a long tradition of practised makers, though how and where they worked is not known. The Nok people also made everyday pottery, whose changing shapes and decoration over some fifteen centuries are the basis of the modern chronology, and they used grinding stones and wore beads of stone. No writing is known from the culture.</p><p>The Nok people were also among the early ironworkers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taruga">Taruga</a>, a trial excavation of eight days in January 1961 uncovered the bases and walls of smelting furnaces still in place, with slag, fragments of tuyères, the clay pipes that carried air into a furnace, and objects of wrought iron; radiocarbon dates from the site point to about the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE. More recent work suggests that iron smelting began in the Nok region during the Middle Nok phase, perhaps between about 750 and 550 BCE, though the dating of early African iron is much debated. Grinding stones and other stone tools remained in use alongside the new metal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Almost nothing is known directly of the Nok people’s dealings with their neighbours. They left no writing, no outside observer described them, and their sites have so far revealed little about trade. What can be said rests on their crops, their technology and their art.</p><p>Pearl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, their staple cereal, had been domesticated in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> by about 2500 BCE, and its presence at Nok sites ties central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> to that northern world. Whether the first Nok farmers brought it with them, as Breunig and Rupp argue, or took it up from neighbours, the beginnings of Nok belong to the wider spread of farming through the savannas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> in the second millennium BCE. The Nok region lies between the dry Sahel to the north and the forests to the south, and not far from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Benue rivers, in later centuries great routes of trade, but whether its people travelled or traded along the rivers is not known.</p><p>Iron raises a larger question. West Africans were smelting iron by the first millennium BCE, and scholars still debate whether the technique was invented in the region, as the historian Christopher Ehret and others have argued, or arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. The furnaces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taruga">Taruga</a> and the dates from other Nok sites are central evidence in that debate but do not settle it. Whatever its origin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> spread among the farming peoples of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the following centuries.</p><p>The Nok style itself is so uniform across a wide area that the communities sharing it must have been in close contact, whether through travelling specialists, the exchange of objects or common rites. Other early terracotta traditions of northern Nigeria, called Sokoto and Katsina after the regions where they were found, share some features with Nok, but they are known mainly from pieces sold on the art market, and their dates and relationship to Nok remain uncertain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Late Nok phase, from about 300 BCE, is known from only a few sites, and around the turn of the Common Era the terracottas cease and the culture disappears from the archaeological record. Why it ended is not known, and what followed in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> over the next thousand years is still poorly understood.</p><p>Many scholars have seen in Nok the beginning of a long tradition of figurative sculpture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Its heads have been compared with the terracottas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-alloy heads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, made between about the 12th and 15th centuries CE, and with the art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, and some writers have suggested that peoples of present-day Nigeria descend in part from the Nok people. More than a thousand years separate Nok from Ife, however, and no continuous chain of finds yet bridges the gap, so such connections remain hypotheses.</p><p>The culture was rediscovered through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mines of the colonial era and first studied by Bernard Fagg (1915–1987), who founded the museum at Jos in 1952 and excavated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taruga">Taruga</a> before becoming curator of the Pitt Rivers Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> in 1963. From the 1970s, and on a vast scale from the mid-1990s, Nok sites were dug up to supply the international art market; most of the known sites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> project’s research area have been looted, and large numbers of sculptures have been smuggled abroad. Nigerian law forbids the unauthorised trade in antiquities, and some pieces have been returned, but many sculptures in collections have no record of where they were found, and forgeries circulate alongside genuine works.</p><p>The Frankfurt project has turned Nok from a set of admired objects into a culture with a history of some fifteen centuries, known from its settlements, food and burials as well as its art. It shows farmers who lived in small hamlets, gathered honey, smelted iron and created an ambitious ritual art, a pattern that does not fit older models in which great art goes with towns and kings.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Nok culture of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, c. 1500 BCE to the turn of the Common Era, was a society of farming hamlets whose people grew pearl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, gathered honey, smelted iron and, from about 900 BCE, made hollow terracotta figures of great skill. Rediscovered through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mining in 1928, it is an early chapter in the history of African sculpture, though looting has destroyed much of its evidence.</em></p>

